  "historical_output_text": " The weather pattern you know well is the daily cycle of day and night. It’s caused by the Earth spinning on its axis, which is like a big ball rotating. Imagine you’re standing on a merry-go-round; as it spins, it exposes different parts of you to the sun at different times. This spinning causes the sun to appear to rise in the east and set in the west, giving us day and night.\n\nDuring the day, the side of the Earth facing the sun heats up. The sun’s heat warms the land and air, causing air to rise. As the air rises, it cools and forms clouds, which can lead to precipitation like rain or snow. This is why it’s often warmer during the day and cools off at night.\n\nAt night, the side of the Earth that’s not facing the sun gets colder because it’s not receiving direct sunlight. The cooler air sinks, bringing in clouds or clearing skies depending on previous conditions. The ground cools down, and the temperature drops, which is why it feels cooler at night.\n\nSo, the Earth’s rotation is the main cause, and it affects the temperature, air movement,",
